792.3,21869.1,21726.4,21881.3,21938.2,22006.4,22084.6,22152.3,22242.5,22476.6,22432.8,22484.3,22753.2,22864.0,22897.3,23047.1,23117.0,23242.5,23339.6],"flow":null,"val":null}],"checks":{"months":813,"blank_months":0,"months_rest_below_zero":0,"stale":false},"source":"OECD monetary aggregates (DSD_STES@DF_MONAGG 4.0): broad money (MABM) and narrow money (MANM) for United States, monthly, national currency and definition, not seasonally adjusted; the rest of broad money is broad minus narrow (our calculation). Licence CC BY 4.0. This is an adaptation of an original work by the OECD. The opinions expressed and arguments employed in this adaptation should not be reported as representing the official views of the OECD or of its Member countries","note":"Broad money is the cash and deposits people and companies hold, plus the savings and other near-money a country's central bank counts; narrow money is the part that can be spent at once, mainly cash and current accounts. This book splits the broad money figure into narrow money and the rest, in the country's own currency, as the OECD collects them each month from the central bank, not seasonally adjusted. Countries draw these lines differently, so amounts are never added across countries or turned into one currency here, and a change in a country's own definition shows as a jump between the two rows without any money moving. The OECD calls the US series M3, although the Federal Reserve stopped publishing M3 in 2006; it follows the Fed's M2 within about 0.7%. In May 2020 the Fed moved savings deposits into narrow money, so narrow money jumps and the rest falls that month. Source: OECD."}